### 5\. GROUP MESSAGING SYSTEM

Group messaging must become a complete extension of the Part 1 messaging foundation.

It must support real communication among multiple users with reliable membership, permissions, privacy, administration and moderation.

### 5.1 Group creation

Allow an eligible user to create a group.

The creation process should support:

* Group name.

* Group description.

* Group avatar.

* Initial participants.

* Privacy setting.

* Invitation permissions.

* Membership approval setting where applicable.

* Relevant group preferences.

Do not overload the creation experience.

Use clear steps, appropriate previews and validation.

The creator becomes the initial authorized administrator according to the group policy.

### 5.2 Group identity

A group should have:

* Stable group ID.

* Display name.

* Description.

* Avatar.

* Creator identity.

* Creation timestamp.

* Membership information.

* Privacy configuration.

* Group status.

* Updated timestamp.

* Last activity summary.

Do not store unnecessary profile snapshots as authoritative user information.

Group display information may be denormalized for performance, but it must not replace the authoritative user profile.

### 5.3 Group membership

Implement:

* Adding authorized members.

* Removing members.

* Leaving a group.

* Joining by invitation.

* Joining through authorized discovery where supported.

* Approval-based membership where configured.

* Rejoining according to group policy.

* Membership status tracking.

* Membership-change history where needed.

Membership operations must be authorized.

A normal member must not be able to remove administrators or modify another member's permissions.

### 5.4 Group invitations

Support secure invitation mechanisms.

Depending on the existing product architecture, these may include:

* Selecting existing eligible users.

* Inviting from a profile or conversation.

* Secure invitation links.

* Invitation expiration.

* Invitation revocation.

* Maximum-use limits where relevant.

* Join requests.

* Invitation acceptance or rejection.

Invitation tokens must be unpredictable and validated on the backend.

Do not expose permanent unrestricted group-join links by default.

Do not allow an unauthorized user to invite people when the group's permissions prohibit it.

### 5.5 Group conversation interface

Extend the established conversation experience.

Support:

* Group avatar.

* Group name.

* Relevant member information.

* Message timeline.

* Sender identities.

* Message timestamps.

* Replies.

* Reactions.

* Forwarding.

* Editing.

* Deletion.

* Attachments.

* Shared Posts, Vibes and Sparks.

* Mentions.

* Read indicators where appropriate.

* Message delivery states.

* Group-specific system events.

* New-message indicators.

Preserve the functionality and visual consistency established in Part 1.

### 5.6 Message attribution

Group messages must clearly identify the sender.

Display:

* Sender name.

* Sender profile image where appropriate.

* Relevant identity or verification status.

* Message content.

* Message timestamp.

* Appropriate interactions.

Do not allow senders to impersonate another member.

Do not trust client-supplied sender information.

Resolve identity through the authoritative profile system.

### 5.7 Group replies and mentions

Support contextual replies and member mentions.

Requirements:

* Reply to a message.

* Preview the original message.

* Open the referenced message where available.

* Mention eligible group participants.

* Validate mention targets.

* Support appropriate mention notifications.

* Avoid notifying every participant for every ordinary message.

* Handle removed or unavailable members.

* Respect notification preferences.

Mention notifications must not grant someone access to a private group.

### 5.8 Group read and delivery states

Group messaging requires a different model from one-to-one delivery.

Do not create a receipt write for every message and every group member without evaluating its cost.

Support appropriate states such as:

* Sent.

* Delivered to relevant participants where technically confirmed.

* Read by specific participants when appropriate and privacy-permitted.

For large groups, use efficient read cursors and carefully bounded receipt data.

Provide a suitable interface for understanding message readership without creating excessive Firestore reads.

Do not imply that all members have read a message merely because one person has.

### 5.9 Group administration

Support role-based administration.

At minimum, design around:

Creator/Owner

The authorized owner of the group.

Administrator

A member with explicitly granted management permissions.

Moderator

A member with restricted moderation capabilities.

Member

A normal participant.

Role names should align with Arvdoul's established role conventions where they already exist.

A group role must not automatically grant global Arvdoul privileges.

### 5.10 Group permission matrix

Implement explicit permissions for:

| Operation                 | Owner        | Admin        | Moderator    | Member       |
| ------------------------- | ------------ | ------------ | ------------ | ------------ |
| View authorized group     | Yes          | Yes          | Yes          | Yes          |
| Send permitted messages   | Yes          | Yes          | Yes          | Yes          |
| Invite members            | Configurable | Configurable | Configurable | Configurable |
| Approve join requests     | Yes          | Configurable | Configurable | No           |
| Remove members            | Yes          | Configurable | Configurable | No           |
| Manage group settings     | Yes          | Configurable | Limited      | No           |
| Assign moderator          | Yes          | Configurable | No           | No           |
| Assign administrator      | Yes          | Restricted   | No           | No           |
| Delete group              | Yes          | No           | No           | No           |
| Moderate messages         | Yes          | Configurable | Configurable | No           |
| Manage group restrictions | Yes          | Configurable | Limited      | No           |

This is a permission design, not a hardcoded assumption that every group must behave identically.

The actual permission configuration must be validated by trusted backend logic.

Prevent privilege escalation.

