### 7\. FOUNDATIONAL VOICE AND VIDEO CALLING

Calls are a significant part of Part 2 and must be treated as genuine real-time infrastructure.

Do not create a fake call interface.

### 7.1 Supported calling scope

Implement a foundational calling system supporting:

* One-to-one audio calls.

* One-to-one video calls.

* Group audio calls.

* Group video calls within a documented and tested capacity.

* Incoming call handling.

* Outgoing call handling.

* Call acceptance.

* Call rejection.

* Call cancellation.

* Call termination.

* Missed-call records.

* Appropriate call notifications.

* Network recovery.

Advanced meetings, screen sharing and advanced call productivity capabilities belong to Part 3.

### 7.2 Infrastructure decision

Before implementation, inspect available real-time infrastructure.

Choose and document a viable approach.

Possible architecture options include:

WebRTC with managed SFU infrastructure

Use a suitable managed real-time media service where it best satisfies reliability, scalability and operational requirements.

WebRTC with self-managed SFU infrastructure

Only choose this when the repository's deployment architecture, operational expertise and hosting capacity make it maintainable.

Existing calling provider

Reuse an existing, properly integrated provider when present.

For reliable multi-party video, evaluate an SFU-based architecture rather than assuming peer-to-peer media connections will scale.

### 7.3 Mandatory production infrastructure

A working calling system must address:

* Signaling.

* ICE negotiation.

* STUN.

* TURN.

* NAT traversal.

* Media transport.

* Audio device access.

* Camera access.

* Call session state.

* Reconnection.

* Participant authorization.

* Call termination.

* Network-quality adaptation.

* Provider outages.

* Abuse prevention.

For multi-party calling, use an architecture that can handle participant media streams without relying on an uncontrolled mesh connection model.

### 7.4 Provider and infrastructure honesty

Do not hardcode undocumented provider credentials.

Do not embed private API keys in frontend code.

Do not pretend TURN or SFU infrastructure exists when it has not been provisioned.

If a required provider, account, credential, server or paid infrastructure dependency is unavailable:

* Identify the specific missing dependency.

* Implement the valid architecture boundary and integration contract.

* Complete the portions that can be tested independently.

* Mark the infrastructure-dependent capability BLOCKED.

* Document the setup needed to activate it.

* Do not substitute a fake call experience.

Prefer the most practical production solution and provide an honest cost and operational comparison.

Avoid unnecessary subscriptions, but do not sacrifice fundamental calling reliability for a superficially free implementation.

### 7.5 Call lifecycle

A logical call lifecycle may include:

```text
initiated
    ↓
ringing
    ↓
accepted
    ↓
connecting
    ↓
connected
    ↓
reconnecting
    ↓
ended
```

Other valid terminal states include:

* Rejected.

* Cancelled.

* Missed.

* Failed.

* Timed out.

Define valid transitions and enforce them through authorized operations.

Do not accept arbitrary client transitions.

### 7.6 Outgoing call

Implement:

* Call initiation.

* Recipient validation.

* Permission checking.

* Call invitation.

* Ringing feedback.

* Waiting state.

* Connection feedback.

* Cancellation.

