### 20\. FIRESTORE AND DATA ARCHITECTURE

Part 2 introduces more complex relationships and larger communication datasets.

Data modeling must remain deliberate and scalable.

The following examples are logical reference structures, not instructions to create duplicate collections when the repository already contains equivalent models.

### 20.1 Group structure

An illustrative model:

```text
groups/{groupId}
    members/{userId}
    invitations/{invitationId}
    joinRequests/{requestId}
    messages/{messageId}
    moderationRecords/{recordId}
    media/{mediaId}
```

### 20.2 Community structure

An illustrative model:

```text
communities/{communityId}
    members/{userId}
    invitations/{invitationId}
    joinRequests/{requestId}
    channels/{channelId}
        messages/{messageId}
        members/{userId}
    moderationRecords/{recordId}
```

Use the actual repository's established organization when it differs.

### 20.3 Calling structure

An illustrative model:

```text
calls/{callId}
    participants/{userId}
    signaling/{signalId}
```

Avoid unbounded signaling documents and excessive persistent writes.

Use ephemeral infrastructure or an appropriate provider for real-time signaling where necessary.

Do not use Firestore as an audio/video transport.

### 20.4 Gift transaction references

An illustrative messaging reference:

```text
giftMessages/{messageId}
    transactionId
    conversationId
    senderId
    recipientId
    createdAt
```

The actual transaction and wallet ledger remain authoritative.

The messaging document is a contextual reference, not a second financial record.

### 20.5 Device records

An illustrative model:

```text
users/{userId}/devices/{deviceId}
```

Store only necessary registration and synchronization metadata.

Follow existing token and session conventions.

### 20.6 Data-model rules

Do not:

* Store unbounded member arrays.

* Store complete message histories in group documents.

* Use one large document for all community channels.

* Put large media in Firestore.

* Expose all member records to unauthorized users.

* Trust client-maintained counts.

* Store sensitive private data redundantly.

* Create a second financial ledger.

* Create a second XP history.

* Make the client authoritative for administrative roles.

Use bounded, indexed and permission-aware queries.

### 20.7 Counters and denormalized data

Member counts, unread counts, channel activity, group summaries and relevant statistics may require denormalization.

Where implemented:

* Define the canonical source.

* Use trusted update operations.

* Protect the counter from arbitrary client writes.

* Handle repeated Cloud Function events.

* Reconcile inconsistencies.

* Document eventual consistency behavior.

Do not invent counts.

