### 26\. PERFORMANCE, COST AND SCALABILITY

Part 2 introduces substantially more users, permissions, streams and communication activity.

### 26.1 Firestore efficiency

Avoid:

* Unbounded group member arrays.

* Unbounded community member arrays.

* Unbounded messages inside a document.

* Unbounded call signaling history.

* Excessive listeners.

* Per-member receipt writes for every large-group message without justification.

* Repeated full profile reads.

* Repeated membership resolution.

* Unnecessary notification fan-out.

* Duplicate XP events.

* Duplicate wallet writes.

Use:

* Bounded queries.

* Cursor pagination.

* Appropriate indexes.

* Efficient membership lookups.

* Controlled listeners.

* Idempotent backend processing.

* Trusted summary updates.

* Appropriate caching.

* Explicit data-retention policies.

### 26.2 Group scaling

Do not assume every group will have the same size.

Document expected group behavior and limits based on the actual product design.

Use appropriate pagination and role resolution.

Do not load every member and every message whenever a group opens.

### 26.3 Community scaling

Community membership and channel histories may become large.

Use:

* Paginated members.

* Paginated messages.

* Efficient channel indexes.

* Appropriate notification fan-out.

* Controlled listeners.

* Server-side moderation operations.

Avoid public counters that trigger large unnecessary reads.

### 26.4 Calling scalability

Evaluate:

* Concurrent sessions.

* Participants per call.

* SFU capacity.

* TURN bandwidth.

* Media quality.

* Regional latency.

* Infrastructure cost.

* Provider limits.

* Failure recovery.

Do not claim global calling scalability without real infrastructure evidence.

### 26.5 XP and Coin cost controls

Use idempotent operations and trusted aggregation.

Prevent repeated Firestore writes for events that should settle only once.

Do not make the messaging UI poll the wallet ledger continuously.

Refresh canonical financial state at meaningful points.

### 26.6 React performance

Avoid rerendering every message when:

* One participant changes presence.

* One reaction changes.

* One membership record changes.

* A call participant updates.

* A notification arrives.

Use targeted state subscriptions and efficient component boundaries.

Use virtualization where justified.

Ensure all listeners, media streams and device resources are properly cleaned up.

### 27\. DATA MIGRATIONS AND BACKWARD COMPATIBILITY

Part 2 may require extending Part 1 data structures.

Every migration must be planned.

Before modifying a schema:

* Identify current readers.

* Identify current writers.

* Identify backend functions.

* Identify security rules.

* Identify relevant indexes.

* Identify existing records.

* Identify older client behavior.

* Define compatibility requirements.

* Design an idempotent migration.

* Define recovery and rollback behavior.

* Test migrated and unmigrated records.

* Document the operational impact.

Do not rename or restructure existing conversation collections unnecessarily.

Do not invalidate message history.

Do not remove Part 1 data simply to achieve architectural uniformity.

Use additive migration wherever possible.

